&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;phase_deg&lt;/code&gt;: phase angle (°)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Notes&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="notes"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#notes" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rows with frequency &amp;lt;= 0 or invalid numeric values are filtered out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If a segment contains no usable data, an error is reported for that segment and it is skipped.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The output columns are fully compatible with &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_chi/"&gt;EIS Extraction: CHI Instruments Data Preprocessing&lt;/a&gt;, so the files can be used directly with &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/plot/"&gt;EIS Plotting: Nyquist and Bode Visualization&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/drt/"&gt;EIS/DRT Analysis: Mapping Frequency-Domain Impedance to a Distribution of Relaxation Times&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</description></item><item><title>EIS Plotting: Nyquist and Bode Visualization</title><link>https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/plot/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/plot/</guid><description>
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://oparic.com/images/workflows/eis_plot.png" alt="EIS Plotting: Nyquist and Bode Visualization" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once you have finished extracting EIS data, the natural first step is to plot the results and assess the data quality.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This workflow batch-plots all extracted &lt;code&gt;*_eis.csv&lt;/code&gt; files as Nyquist and Bode diagrams, letting you quickly spot sample differences, outliers, and measurement stability. It also exports an Origin project file so you can refine and publish figures later.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Prerequisites&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="prerequisites"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#prerequisites" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Run &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_chi/"&gt;EIS Extraction: CHI Instruments Data Preprocessing&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_in_situ_dh/"&gt;In-Situ EIS Extraction: DH Instruments&lt;/a&gt; first and make sure the directory already contains standardized &lt;code&gt;*_eis.csv&lt;/code&gt; files.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Procedure&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="procedure"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#procedure" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Select the input directory&lt;/strong&gt;: choose the output directory from &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_chi/"&gt;EIS Extraction: CHI Instruments Data Preprocessing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The system automatically detects all &lt;code&gt;*_eis.csv&lt;/code&gt; files and batch-plots them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The output directory is the input directory itself; once processing is complete you can view images and project files directly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What Gets Generated&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="what-gets-generated"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#what-gets-generated" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h3&gt;1) Nyquist Plot&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="1-nyquist-plot"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#1-nyquist-plot" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;x-axis: &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;msup&gt;&lt;mi&gt;Z&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em"&gt;′&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;/msup&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;Z&amp;#x27;&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.7519em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.07153em;"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:0.7519em;"&gt;&lt;span style="top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;y-axis: &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mo&gt;−&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;msup&gt;&lt;mi&gt;Z&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mo mathvariant="normal"&gt;′&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mo mathvariant="normal"&gt;′&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;/msup&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;-Z&amp;#x27;&amp;#x27;&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.8352em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;−&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.07153em;"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:0.7519em;"&gt;&lt;span style="top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;′′&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Useful for observing charge-transfer impedance, diffusion behavior, and the overall shape before equivalent-circuit fitting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2) Bode Plot&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="2-bode-plot"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#2-bode-plot" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Magnitude plot: &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi mathvariant="normal"&gt;∣&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi&gt;Z&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi mathvariant="normal"&gt;∣&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;|Z|&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;∣&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.07153em;"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;∣&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; vs. frequency (log scale)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Phase plot: Phase vs. frequency (log scale)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Convenient for comparing response mechanisms across different frequency ranges&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Output&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="output"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#output" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Each sample produces:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;*_nyquist_matplotlib.png&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;*_bode_matplotlib.png&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;*_eis_plots.opju&lt;/code&gt; (single-sample Origin project)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In addition, a merged file is generated:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;eis_plots_merged.opju&lt;/code&gt; (multi-sample merged Origin project)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Subsequent Analysis&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="subsequent-analysis"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#subsequent-analysis" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/drt/"&gt;EIS/DRT Analysis: Mapping Frequency-Domain Impedance to a Distribution of Relaxation Times&lt;/a&gt;: perform DRT analysis on the same set of &lt;code&gt;*_eis.csv&lt;/code&gt; files&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</description></item><item><title>EIS/DRT Analysis: Mapping Frequency-Domain Impedance to a Distribution of Relaxation Times</title><link>https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/drt/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/drt/</guid><description>
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://oparic.com/images/workflows/eis_drt.png" alt="EIS/DRT Analysis: Mapping Frequency-Domain Impedance to a Distribution of Relaxation Times" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you have already reviewed the Nyquist and Bode plots but want to understand which kinetic process is dominating the response, this workflow is designed for exactly that purpose.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It performs DRT (Distribution of Relaxation Times) analysis on extracted EIS data, mapping frequency-domain impedance information onto a time-constant distribution. This makes it much easier to distinguish different processes visually (e.g., interfacial charge transfer, diffusion-related processes, etc.).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Prerequisites&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="prerequisites"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#prerequisites" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Run &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_chi/"&gt;EIS Extraction: CHI Instruments Data Preprocessing&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/extract_in_situ_dh/"&gt;In-Situ EIS Extraction: DH Instruments&lt;/a&gt; first and ensure the input consists of standardized EIS CSVs containing &lt;code&gt;freq_hz&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;z_real_ohm&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;z_imag_ohm&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Procedure&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="procedure"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#procedure" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;You have two options:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Select a directory (batch-process all &lt;code&gt;.csv&lt;/code&gt; files in it)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Or select a single &lt;code&gt;.csv&lt;/code&gt; file (process one sample only)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The program automatically creates a &lt;code&gt;drt_output&lt;/code&gt; directory and outputs results for each sample separately.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Analysis and Fitting Results&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="analysis-and-fitting-results"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#analysis-and-fitting-results" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Each sample produces the following data files:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;fit_data.csv&lt;/code&gt;: experimental impedance, DRT-reconstructed impedance, and residuals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;drt_data.csv&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;\tau&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.4306em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;γ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;(&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;)&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;\gamma(\tau)&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.05556em;"&gt;γ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; distribution&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;summary.json&lt;/code&gt;: regularization parameters and solver summary (e.g., &lt;code&gt;lambda_value&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;epsilon&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Along with the following visualization plots:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;nyquist_fit.png&lt;/code&gt;: experimental vs. DRT-fitted Nyquist comparison&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;drt_tau.png&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;γ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;\gamma&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.05556em;"&gt;γ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;ndash;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;\tau&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.4306em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; distribution plot (log-scale x-axis)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;residuals.png&lt;/code&gt;: real/imaginary residual plot&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;*_drt_plots.opju&lt;/code&gt;: Origin project (containing Nyquist, DRT, and residual plots)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Principle (Brief)&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="principle-brief"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#principle-brief" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;DRT represents impedance as a superposition of processes with different time constants. The core idea can be written as:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;span class="katex-display"&gt;&lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;Z&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;(&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;ω&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;)&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mo&gt;=&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;msub&gt;&lt;mi&gt;R&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi mathvariant="normal"&gt;∞&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/msub&gt;&lt;mo&gt;+&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mo&gt;∫&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mfrac&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;γ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;(&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;)&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mn&gt;1&lt;/mn&gt;&lt;mo&gt;+&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;j&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi&gt;ω&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;/mfrac&gt;&lt;mtext&gt; &lt;/mtext&gt;&lt;mi&gt;d&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mi&gt;ln&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo&gt;⁡&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;Z(\omega)=R_\infty + \int \frac{\gamma(\tau)}{1+j\omega\tau} \, d\ln\tau&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.07153em;"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.03588em;"&gt;ω&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mrel"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.00773em;"&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="msupsub"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:0.1514em;"&gt;&lt;span style="top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0077em;margin-right:0.05em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:2.7em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mtight"&gt;∞&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:0.15em;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mbin"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:2.3074em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right:0.44445em;position:relative;top:-0.0011em;"&gt;∫&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.1667em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mopen nulldelimiter"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mfrac"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:1.427em;"&gt;&lt;span style="top:-2.314em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:3em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mbin"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.03588em;"&gt;jω&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="top:-3.23em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:3em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width:0.04em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="top:-3.677em;"&gt;&lt;span class="pstrut" style="height:3em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord"&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.05556em;"&gt;γ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-s"&gt;​&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="vlist-r"&gt;&lt;span class="vlist" style="height:0.8804em;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose nulldelimiter"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.1667em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.1667em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mop"&gt;ln&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.1667em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;p&gt;Where:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The peak positions of &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;γ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;(&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;mi&gt;τ&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;mo stretchy="false"&gt;)&lt;/mo&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;\gamma(\tau)&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="base"&gt;&lt;span class="strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.05556em;"&gt;γ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mopen"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1132em;"&gt;τ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mclose"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; reflect the characteristic time scales of different processes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The peak area and height relate to the contribution strength of each process&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This approach makes it significantly easier to resolve overlapping mechanisms compared to interpreting Nyquist semicircles alone.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;FAQ&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="faq"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#faq" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why are there no results for some samples?&lt;/strong&gt; The most common reason is missing input columns or invalid values in the data. The program skips these samples and reports the specific error.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Practical Tips&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="practical-tips"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#practical-tips" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;If this is your first time running DRT, it is a good idea to use &lt;a href="https://oparic.com/en/docs/eis/plot/"&gt;EIS Plotting: Nyquist and Bode Visualization&lt;/a&gt; first to verify that the curves are smooth and free of obvious outliers. Doing so before entering this step typically yields more stable and more interpretable results.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>